{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>El Shaddai</b> — two names, each an attribute on its own. The meaning of \"El\" is \"mighty,\" from the language of \"אֵילֵי מוֹאָב\" (Exodus 15:15). The meaning of \"Shaddai\" — Rashi said: that there is sufficiency (דַּי) in His divinity for every creature. And in The Guide for the Perplexed (I:63), the Rav explained it to mean that He has no need for the existence of that which exists, nor for the continuance of anything else's existence, but His existence suffices for itself. And Rabbi Abraham explained it in the name of the Nagid, of blessed memory, as derived from שׁוֹדֵד (devastator), meaning: overcoming and devastating the arrays of the heavens. And this is correct, for it is the attribute of Gevurah (Might) that governs the world, which the Sages call \"the attribute of judgment below\" (Genesis Rabbah 35:4). And the reason this name is mentioned now is that through it the hidden miracles performed for the righteous are done — to save from death of soul and to keep them alive in famine and to redeem them in war from the power of the sword — like all the miracles performed for Abraham and the Patriarchs, and like all that appear in the Torah in the portion of \"If in My statutes\" (Leviticus 26:3–46) and in the portion of \"And it shall come to pass when you come\" (Deuteronomy 28:1–68), in the blessings and the curses — all of these are miracles, for it is not in the nature of things that the rains come in their season because we serve Elohim, nor that the heavens become as iron in the seventh year of sowing; and so too all the promises in the Torah — rather, they are all miracles, and in all of them the array of the constellations is overcome, except that they do not involve a change in the way of the world like the miracles performed through Moses our teacher — the ten plagues, the splitting of the sea, the manna, the well, and others — which are wonders that change nature publicly; those are performed through the special Name that He had made known to him. Therefore He now says to Abraham our father that He is the Mighty One, the Overcomer, who will prevail over his constellation and bring him to beget, and there will be a covenant between Him and his seed forever — that HaShem's portion is His people, and by His will He guides them; they shall not be under the dominion of star or constellation. And know and see that Abraham our father never mentioned the Name יוֹ\"ד ה' (the Tetragrammaton) in his speech except in combination with the Name written in אָלֶ\"ף דָּלֶ\"ת (Adonai) or in combination with El Elyon together with it; and he mentions \"Elohim\" in his affairs, and so he says \"YHWH, Elohim of the heavens\" (Genesis 24:7), and he said \"YHWH will see\" (Genesis 22:14) concerning the place of the future Temple, and Jacob always mentioned \"El Shaddai\" (below 43:14, 48:3), while Moses our teacher never does so. And if you attend carefully, you will understand all of this from what they said in tractate Yevamot (Yevamot 49b): all the prophets gazed through an obscuring speculum, and therefore Isaiah said, \"And I saw Adonai\" (Isaiah 6:1), and Moses gazed through a luminous speculum, and therefore he said, \"For no man shall see Me and live\" (Exodus 33:20). \"And I saw Adonai\" is written with אָלֶ\"ף דָּלֶ\"ף (Adonai). And I will explain this further in the portion of Va'era (Exodus 6:2), if Elohim sees fit to grant me the opportunity." "<b>Walk before Me</b> — to go in the way that I shall instruct you, in the manner of \"After HaShem your Elohim you shall walk, and Him you shall fear\" (Deuteronomy 13:5). For one who commands to grasp his way before he teaches it will say, \"Walk before Me,\" and one who comes after the commandment will say, \"After HaShem you shall walk.\" And the meaning of both is to walk after HaShem, to fear Him alone and to do what He commands." "<b>And be wholehearted</b> — another commandment yet in this matter, in the manner of \"You shall be wholehearted with HaShem your Elohim\" (Deuteronomy 18:13), following the warning of the verse \"There shall not be found among you a diviner of divinations, a soothsayer, an augur, or a sorcerer\" (ibid. 18:10). And the meaning of both is that one should believe in his heart that the Holy One, blessed be He, alone is the Master of the capacity — in the beginning and in the end; He is the One who has power to do and to annul. And they should not heed soothsayers or diviners or an augur or sorcerer, nor believe that their words will come about in any case; rather, one should resolve in his heart that everything is in the hand of the Supreme of the supremes — that He is \"El\" and He is \"El Shaddai,\" bringing good that was not in the constellation and bringing evil when the constellation is good and fair, according as a person walks before Him — \"frustrating the omens of liars, and making diviners mad\" (Isaiah 44:25). And this is what they said (Shabbat 156a): \"Step out of your astrology,\" and so forth. And Rashi explained (Rashi on Genesis 17:1): \"And be wholehearted\" — be whole in all the trials. And Rabbi Abraham said that he should not ask about the circumcision, \"Why?\" — and it is in the manner of \"Let my heart be whole in Your statutes, that I be not ashamed\" (Psalms 119:80). And the correct interpretation is what I have explained."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {0 "The closing phrase אִם יִרְאֶה אֱלֹהִים בְּעָנְיִי is translated hyper-literally; it is a formulaic expression of humility about continuing to a future commentary section, literally 'if Elohim will see [fit] in my affair.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:30:49.198860778Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}